Recognition: a fixed-size window needs only a [[o:contiguous range]] and an updateable aggregate; it does not require monotonicity.

A variable-size window needs a [[g:monotone validity predicate]]: after expansion, moving the left boundary must be able to restore validity without revisiting earlier positions. Under that precondition the pointers never move backward, giving O(n) state transitions.

**Variable-size (dynamic)** — the standard template:

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> left = <span class="n">0</span>, best = <span class="n">0</span>;
<span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> right = <span class="n">0</span>; right &lt; n; right++) {
    add(a[right]);                      <span class="c">// expand on the right</span>
    <span class="k">while</span> (!valid()) {                 <span class="c">// invalid =&gt; shrink from the left</span>
        remove(a[left]);
        left++;
    }
    best = Math.<span class="f">max</span>(best, right - left + <span class="n">1</span>);
}</code></pre>

**Fixed-size (constant k)** — no `while` is needed; just slide:

<pre><code><span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> right = <span class="n">0</span>; right &lt; n; right++) {
    add(a[right]);
    <span class="k">if</span> (right &gt;= k) remove(a[right - k]);   <span class="c">// remove the element that fell out</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> (right &gt;= k - <span class="n">1</span>) record(left = right - k + <span class="n">1</span>);
}</code></pre>

Each element enters the window exactly once and leaves exactly once → [[g:O(n)]], even with a `while` nested inside the `for`. Interviewers often probe this point: *"Why is a while loop nested inside a for loop still O(n)?"*

:::deep
- The [[r:necessary]] condition for sliding window to work is that the property be monotonic with respect to window length. An array with [[r:negative numbers]] breaks that condition for "subarray sum ≥ k" → use prefix sums plus a deque or binary search, not sliding window.
- Shrinking with `while` versus `if`: use `while` for a "*longest* valid window" problem; use `if` for a fixed-size window.
- Window state is typically a frequency-counting `HashMap`, a running sum, or a monotonic `Deque` for window maximum/minimum problems.
- The "exactly k distinct characters" variant = `atMost(k) - atMost(k-1)` — a useful and frequently tested trick.
- Classic progression by difficulty: LC 3 (no repeated characters) → LC 76 (minimum window substring) → LC 30 (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q3) → LC 239 (sliding window maximum, which requires a deque).

**Precondition check:** for a variable window, write the predicate and its monotonicity before the loop.

With non-negative values, once `sum >= target`, removing from the left cannot increase `sum`; with arbitrary negatives that reasoning is unavailable. Fixed-size windows need boundary checks and an updateable aggregate, not this monotonicity proof.
:::

**Canonical problem:** count the subarrays whose sum is exactly `k`, where the array [[r:may contain negative numbers]].

Sliding window [[r:fails]] here: with negative numbers, expanding the window no longer increases the sum monotonically → there is no reliable signal for when to shrink it.

**Idea:** let `P[i]` be the prefix sum through `i`. The subarray `(j, i]` has sum `k` ⟺ `P[i] − P[j] = k` ⟺ `P[j] = P[i] − k`. We therefore only need to count how many times we have already seen the value `P[i] − k`.

<pre><code><span class="k">long</span> <span class="f">subarraySum</span>(<span class="k">int</span>[] a, <span class="k">int</span> k) {
    Map&lt;Long,Integer&gt; seen = <span class="k">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    seen.<span class="f">put</span>(<span class="n">0L</span>, <span class="n">1</span>);              <span class="c">// empty prefix: important!</span>
    <span class="k">long</span> sum = <span class="n">0L</span>, count = <span class="n">0L</span>;
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> x : a) {
        sum += x;
        count += seen.<span class="f">getOrDefault</span>(sum - k, <span class="n">0</span>);
        seen.<span class="f">merge</span>(sum, <span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum);
    }
    <span class="k">return</span> count;
}</code></pre>

O(n) time and O(n) space. The `seen.put(0, 1)` line handles the case where the prefix from the beginning already sums to `k` — [[r:forget it and the first test case fails immediately]].

:::deep
The same template produces several other solutions by changing what you store or count:

- **Sum divisible by k** → store `sum % k` (remember to normalize negative values: `((sum % k) + k) % k`)
- **Longest subarray with sum = k** → map each prefix sum to the [[o:first index]] where it appears, and insert only when it is absent
- **Equal numbers of 0s and 1s** → convert every 0 to −1, reducing the problem to sum = 0
- **Parity and XOR problems** → use prefix XOR with the analogous equation `P[i] ^ P[j] = k`

- In 2D, use two-dimensional prefix sums + inclusion–exclusion for O(1) rectangle-sum queries.
- If dynamic updates are required, replace the prefix array with a [[o:Fenwick tree / BIT]] so both queries and updates are O(log n).
- [[r:Integer overflow]]: summing 10⁵ elements as large as 10⁹ exceeds `int` → use `long`. Interviewers frequently probe this exact issue.
- State the boundary clearly: sliding window for [[g:non-negative arrays]], prefix sum + hash map for [[g:arrays that may contain negatives]]. Articulating this distinction is a strong signal.

The sentinel is `P[0] = 0`, the prefix before index 0; it counts a match whose left boundary is 0. Use a numeric type that cannot overflow for every allowed prefix, and a count type that can hold up to `n*(n+1)/2` matches. Hash-map O(n) is expected under normal hashing, not a worst-case guarantee.
:::

Both explore a state space; the difference is what the problem asks you to reuse or enumerate.

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Dynamic programming</th><th>Backtracking</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Signal</td><td>overlapping subproblems plus an optimal/count/feasible result</td><td>enumerate or find assignments under constraints</td></tr>
<tr><td>Core</td><td>cache one result per sufficient state</td><td>choose → recurse → undo, pruning invalid branches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complexity</td><td>reachable states × transition cost</td><td>size of the explored search tree, often output-sensitive</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

For DP, write five things before code: state meaning, transition, base cases, evaluation order, and where the answer lives. Start top-down when it exposes the recurrence; convert to bottom-up when stack depth, iteration order, or memory locality matters.

Compress a dimension only after proving future transitions no longer need overwritten states. Store a predecessor/choice when the problem asks for the actual path, not just its score.

<pre><code><span class="c">// Top-down: state must contain every fact that affects future choices.</span>
long <span class="f">solve</span>(State s) {
    <span class="k">if</span> (s.<span class="f">done</span>()) <span class="k">return</span> s.<span class="f">baseValue</span>();
    <span class="k">if</span> (memo.<span class="f">containsKey</span>(s)) <span class="k">return</span> memo.<span class="f">get</span>(s);
    long best = IDENTITY;
    <span class="k">for</span> (Choice c : s.<span class="f">choices</span>())
        best = <span class="f">combine</span>(best, <span class="f">solve</span>(s.<span class="f">take</span>(c)));
    memo.<span class="f">put</span>(s, best);
    <span class="k">return</span> best;
}</code></pre>

For backtracking, define the partial assignment and invariant, generate only legal choices, apply one choice, recurse, then undo exactly that mutation. Sort when it enables duplicate skipping or monotone pruning.

If two paths reach the same sufficient state and only the best/count result matters, memoization often turns the search into DP.

:::deep
Common mistakes are memoizing an incomplete state, using a mutable collection as a map key, pruning without a proof, sharing a result list that later mutations corrupt, and claiming `O(2^n)` when pruning or output size dominates.

Validate the recurrence on a tiny brute-force oracle, include empty/minimal cases, and test reconstruction separately from the optimal value.

Memoization removes repeated work only when the key contains every fact that affects continuation.

DP cost is reachable states times transition cost; backtracking cost includes every emitted solution and the cost of copying it. A pruning rule needs a proof that no discarded branch can produce a required result.
:::

Use a [[g:monotonic structure]] when an element that is worse than a newer element can never become useful again.

- A decreasing stack answers next/previous greater, stock span, and histogram-boundary questions. State whether it stores values or indices and whether equal values are popped; that equality choice defines boundaries and duplicate behavior.
- When an item is popped, the current index often finalizes its answer.
- A monotonic deque handles a moving window. Remove expired indices from the front, remove dominated candidates from the back, then add the new index. The front is the best live candidate. Every index enters and leaves at most once, giving amortized `O(n)`.

<pre><code>Deque&lt;Integer&gt; dq = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
<span class="k">for</span> (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
    <span class="k">while</span> (!dq.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>() &amp;&amp; dq.<span class="f">peekFirst</span>() &lt;= i - k) dq.<span class="f">removeFirst</span>();
    <span class="k">while</span> (!dq.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>() &amp;&amp; a[dq.<span class="f">peekLast</span>()] &lt;= a[i]) dq.<span class="f">removeLast</span>();
    dq.<span class="f">addLast</span>(i);
}</code></pre>

For intervals, first define endpoint semantics. Half-open `[start,end)` intervals make adjacent ranges non-overlapping and match many time APIs. Sorting by start supports merge/insert/meeting-room scans.

A sweep line converts ranges into ordered start/end events or deltas; maintain the active count/state between events. For equal coordinates, event ordering must follow the endpoint definition—do not rely on an accidental comparator tie.

:::deep
Recognize the shared invariant: discard state only after proving it cannot affect a future answer. For stacks/deques that proof is dominance; for merged intervals it is coverage; for sweep lines it is that all events before the cursor are finalized.

Use `long` for coordinates/deltas when subtraction can overflow. With a huge sparse coordinate space, sort events or coordinate-compress; with a small bounded space, a difference array may be simpler. Test equal values, zero-length/adjacent intervals, duplicate endpoints, `k=1`, `k=n`, and empty input.

Make duplicate policy executable: `&lt;=` in this deque code removes an older equal candidate, so the newest equal index wins; using `&lt;` keeps older equal candidates.

For a stack, strict versus non-strict comparison changes which equal endpoint is assigned. State whether intervals are closed or half-open; zero-length and touching ranges follow that choice.
:::

Start with what must flow [[g:down]] from ancestors, what must flow [[g:up]] from children, and whether processing is preorder, inorder, postorder, or level order. DFS suits subtree aggregation and path state; BFS suits levels and unweighted distance.

Recursive DFS uses the call stack—on a degenerate tree, use an explicit stack or another strategy if depth can exceed safe limits.

For a BST, the invariant is global: every key in the left subtree belongs in the permitted lower range and every key in the right subtree in the upper range. Checking only `node.left &lt; node &lt; node.right` misses a violation deeper in a subtree.

Carry exclusive/inclusive bounds according to an explicit duplicate policy, and use a wide type or nullable bounds instead of computing `value ± 1` near numeric limits. Plain BST operations are `O(h)`, which becomes `O(n)` when unbalanced; `O(log n)` requires a balance guarantee.

<pre><code>boolean <span class="f">valid</span>(Node n, Long low, Long high) {
    <span class="k">if</span> (n == null) <span class="k">return</span> true;
    <span class="k">if</span> (low != null &amp;&amp; n.key &lt;= low) <span class="k">return</span> false;
    <span class="k">if</span> (high != null &amp;&amp; n.key &gt;= high) <span class="k">return</span> false;
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">valid</span>(n.left, low, (long) n.key)
        &amp;&amp; <span class="f">valid</span>(n.right, (long) n.key, high);
}</code></pre>

A trie trades memory for prefix operations. Each edge represents a symbol; nodes need an end-of-key marker because a key can prefix another.

Choose child storage from the alphabet and workload: fixed arrays are fast but sparse, maps save sparse space, compressed/radix tries collapse one-child chains. Define normalization, case, and Unicode code-point/grapheme semantics before coding.

:::deep
Many “tree problems” are tree DP: return a small summary such as height, validity, best downward path, and best subtree answer from each child instead of rescanning subtrees. If the input can contain shared nodes or cycles, it is a graph, so ownership and a visited policy matter.

Test empty/single/skewed trees, duplicate policy, numeric boundaries, prefix-as-key, and non-ASCII input.

BST validation is a global-bound invariant, not a child comparison.

Choose strict or inclusive bounds for duplicates and carry them without `value ± 1`; use iterative traversal when depth is external or unbounded.

A trie is prefix-correct only after alphabet, normalization, and symbol unit (ASCII, code point, or grapheme) are fixed.
:::

**Size the problem first.** Ten million 64-bit keys in a sorted array are 80 MB; a `HashSet&lt;Long&gt;` is several times that. Exact membership does not fit, so memory answers "definitely not / probably yes", and only a "probably" pays for an exact check.

**A Bloom filter, sized by arithmetic rather than habit:**
<pre><code>m        = 10 MiB × 8                  = 83,886,080 bits
m / n                                  ≈ 8.4 bits per key
best k   = (m/n) · ln 2                ≈ 5.8  → use 6 positions
FP rate  ≈ (1 − e^(−k·n/m))^k          ≈ 1.8 %
<span class="r">k = 3 ("hash it three times")</span>           ≈ 2.7 %</code></pre>

Six positions do not need six hash functions: two 64-bit hashes combined as `h1 + i·h2` give the same false-positive rate (Kirsch–Mitzenmacher).

The whole filter is one `long[]` and one hash mix:
<pre><code>final class Bloom {
  private static final int K = 6;
  private final long[] words = new long[1_310_720];     // 10 MiB = 83,886,080 bits
  private final long m = 64L * words.length;
  void add(long key) {
    long h1 = mix(key), h2 = mix(h1) | 1;               // odd step: the K probes never coincide
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; K; i++) {
      long bit = Long.remainderUnsigned(h1 + i * h2, m);
      words[(int) (bit >>> 6)] |= 1L &lt;&lt; bit;            // a long shift uses the low 6 bits
    }
  }
  boolean mightContain(long key) {
    long h1 = mix(key), h2 = mix(h1) | 1;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; K; i++) {
      long bit = Long.remainderUnsigned(h1 + i * h2, m);
      if ((words[(int) (bit >>> 6)] &amp; (1L &lt;&lt; bit)) == 0) return false;   // one 0 bit is proof
    }
    return true;                                        // only "probably": confirm on disk
  }
  private static long mix(long z) {                     // splitmix64 finalizer, a stand-in for xxHash
    z = (z ^ (z >>> 30)) * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9L;
    z = (z ^ (z >>> 27)) * 0x94D049BB133111EBL;
    return z ^ (z >>> 31);
  }
}</code></pre>

**Because the keys never change, a static filter does better.** A binary fuse filter stores one fingerprint per key in about 1.13 × its width; 8.4 bits per key fits a 7-bit fingerprint — about 0.8% false positives, and three or four memory reads per lookup instead of six.

**The exact check for a "probably":** sort the keys once on disk (external sort — sorted runs, then a k-way merge), keep every 4,096th key in memory as a sparse index (about 2,450 entries), and one page read confirms or rejects the key.

:::tip What the interviewer is testing
That you size before you choose, that "no false negatives" is the property doing the work, and that you can say what the false-positive rate costs downstream.
:::

:::deep
- a Bloom filter cannot delete and degrades as keys are added past its design `n` — harmless here, which is why the question says the keys never change.
- if the keys are dense integers in a known range (IDs 0..80M, say), a plain bitset is exact: one bit per possible value, 80M bits = 10 MB. Ask what the keys look like before reaching for hashing.
- the false-positive rate is a load number: at 1.8%, a million lookups per second for absent keys send about 18,000 per second to the exact check. That sizes the disk tier, not the filter.
- this is the mechanism inside LSM stores — RocksDB and Cassandra keep a filter per SSTable so a point read skips files that cannot hold the key (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q7) — and caches use one against penetration (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q3).
- hash quality matters more than count: use a well-mixed 64-bit hash (xxHash, Murmur3). `Long.hashCode()` folds the value to 32 bits with an XOR and is far too regular for this.
- in a scaled-out service the filter is built once offline and shipped as a file; every replica memory-maps the same bytes instead of rebuilding them.
:::

The array is [[g:sorted]], so the sum at `(L,R)` tells you which pointer to move — and each move [[o:permanently discards]] a whole set of pairs. That is why one pass suffices.

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='two-pointers'></figure>

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> l = <span class="n">0</span>, r = a.length - <span class="n">1</span>;
<span class="k">while</span> (l &lt; r) {
    <span class="k">int</span> sum = a[l] + a[r];
    <span class="k">if</span> (sum == target) <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">new</span> <span class="k">int</span>[]{l, r};
    <span class="k">if</span> (sum &lt; target) l++;      <span class="c">// need bigger  &#8594; only L can help</span>
    <span class="k">else</span>                r--;      <span class="c">// need smaller &#8594; only R can help</span>
}</code></pre>

**The three shapes** this pattern takes: [[g:converging]] (above), [[g:fast/slow]] (cycle detection, middle of a list), and [[g:same-direction]] read/write (in-place dedupe, where the write pointer lags the read pointer).

:::tip Recognition
Sorted array plus "find a pair/triplet", or "remove/partition in place", or "is there a cycle". The deeper explanation of variants is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q4).

This trace is valid only under the sorted-order (or equivalent monotone-feasibility) precondition. Without it, moving `L` or `R` can discard a pair that is still a solution. If values may overflow `int`, use a wider sum type before applying the same invariant.
:::

A window slides by [[g:one add and one remove]] — never by recomputing the whole window. That is the entire trick: O(1) work per step instead of O(k).

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='sliding-window'></figure>

<pre><code><span class="k">long</span> sum = <span class="n">0</span>, best = Long.MIN_VALUE;
<span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> r = <span class="n">0</span>; r &lt; n; r++) {
    sum += a[r];                          <span class="c">// the element sliding in</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> (r &gt;= k) sum -= a[r - k];          <span class="c">// the one falling out</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> (r &gt;= k - <span class="n">1</span>) best = Math.<span class="f">max</span>(best, sum);
}</code></pre>

For a [[o:variable-size]] window the shape changes: expand `right` always, and `while` the window is invalid, shrink `left`. The full template plus the monotonicity requirement is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q2).

:::warn The condition people forget
Sliding window needs the property to be [[r:monotonic in window length]]. With negative numbers, "subarray sum ≥ k" is not — shrinking can *increase* the sum, so the window is unsound. Use prefix sums instead (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q7).
:::

Every comparison [[g:eliminates half the remaining candidates]]. Correctness comes from one invariant: the answer, if it exists, is always inside `[lo, hi]`.

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='binary-search'></figure>

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> lo = <span class="n">0</span>, hi = n - <span class="n">1</span>;              <span class="c">// inclusive bounds</span>
<span class="k">while</span> (lo &lt;= hi) {                     <span class="c">// &lt;= : the 1-element case still runs</span>
    <span class="k">int</span> mid = lo + (hi - lo) / <span class="n">2</span>;      <span class="c">// overflow-safe</span>
    <span class="k">if</span>      (a[mid] == target) <span class="k">return</span> mid;
    <span class="k">else</span> <span class="k">if</span> (a[mid] &lt;  target) lo = mid + <span class="n">1</span>;
    <span class="k">else</span>                      hi = mid - <span class="n">1</span>;
}
<span class="k">return</span> -<span class="n">1</span>;</code></pre>

**The version worth memorising is not this one** — it is the [[o:boundary search]] (`lowerBound`): find the first index where a predicate turns true. It answers "insert position", "first bad version" and "count of x" without any of the off-by-one debates.

<pre><code><span class="c">// smallest i in [0,n] with ok(i) true; assumes ok is false...false,true...true</span>
<span class="k">int</span> lo = <span class="n">0</span>, hi = n;                    <span class="c">// half-open: hi is a valid answer</span>
<span class="k">while</span> (lo &lt; hi) {
    <span class="k">int</span> mid = lo + (hi - lo) / <span class="n">2</span>;
    <span class="k">if</span> (<span class="f">ok</span>(mid)) hi = mid;             <span class="c">// keep mid as a candidate</span>
    <span class="k">else</span>          lo = mid + <span class="n">1</span>;
}
<span class="k">return</span> lo;                            <span class="c">// == n means "no such index"</span></code></pre>

:::deep
- **Termination**, the thing that actually causes infinite loops: every branch must shrink the range. `lo = mid` without `+1` loops forever when `hi == lo+1`. Pick one style — inclusive `[lo,hi]` with `lo&lt;=hi`, or half-open `[lo,hi)` with `lo&lt;hi` — and never mix them.
- The array is not the only thing you can search. Binary searching the *answer space* turns "minimise the maximum" into a feasibility check; see (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q3).
- Rotated arrays: compare `a[mid]` against `a[lo]`/`a[hi]` to decide which half is sorted, then binary search that half. Duplicates degrade it to O(n) worst case.
- In Java, `Arrays.binarySearch` returns `-(insertionPoint) - 1` when absent — the negative encoding carries the boundary, so `-result - 1` is the insert position.

For answer-space search, replace “the array is sorted” with a written contract: `ok(x)` is false then true over an ordered domain, and `[lo, hi]` brackets the first true value. If that predicate is not monotone, this loop can return a plausible but wrong value.
:::

Spiral order is not a clever formula — it is [[g:four boundaries that close inward]]. Keep `top/bottom/left/right` and walk one edge at a time, shrinking after each.

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='matrix-traversal'></figure>

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> top = <span class="n">0</span>, bot = m - <span class="n">1</span>, left = <span class="n">0</span>, right = n - <span class="n">1</span>;
List&lt;Integer&gt; out = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
<span class="k">while</span> (top &lt;= bot &amp;&amp; left &lt;= right) {
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> c = left; c &lt;= right; c++) out.<span class="f">add</span>(g[top][c]);
    top++;
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> r = top; r &lt;= bot; r++) out.<span class="f">add</span>(g[r][right]);
    right--;
    <span class="k">if</span> (top &lt;= bot) {                                   <span class="c">// guard: row not consumed</span>
        <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> c = right; c &gt;= left; c--) out.<span class="f">add</span>(g[bot][c]);
        bot--;
    }
    <span class="k">if</span> (left &lt;= right) {                                <span class="c">// guard: col not consumed</span>
        <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> r = bot; r &gt;= top; r--) out.<span class="f">add</span>(g[r][left]);
        left++;
    }
}</code></pre>

**The two guards are the whole interview.** Without them, a 1×n or n×1 remainder gets visited twice — and it only shows up on non-square input, which is exactly the test case people skip.

:::deep
- **Related matrix moves**, all boundary arithmetic rather than tricks:
  - **transpose then reverse each row** = rotate 90° clockwise, in place.
  - **`r*n + c`** flattens 2-D to 1-D, so a row-sorted matrix binary searches as one array.
  - **mark the first row/column** as dirty flags for set-matrix-zeroes in O(1) extra space.
- A grid is also a graph: neighbours are `{{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}}`, and island/flood-fill problems are plain BFS/DFS (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q13). Reach for the traversal, not a bespoke loop.
- Always bound-check before dereferencing (`0 <= r < m && 0 <= c < n`) — in Java the reward for forgetting is `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` rather than the silent corruption you would get in C.

The four boundaries are inclusive: after each move, the unvisited rectangle is the only remaining region. The guards prevent a single row or column from being emitted twice; for an empty matrix, return before initializing `bottom` and `right` from its dimensions.
:::

The three depth-first orders differ only in [[g:when you visit the node]] relative to its subtrees. Everything else is identical.

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='tree-traversal'></figure>

<pre><code><span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">inorder</span>(Node t) {
    <span class="k">if</span> (t == <span class="k">null</span>) <span class="k">return</span>;
    <span class="f">inorder</span>(t.left);      <span class="c">// everything smaller, in order</span>
    <span class="f">visit</span>(t);             <span class="c">// move this line to change the traversal</span>
    <span class="f">inorder</span>(t.right);     <span class="c">// everything larger, in order</span>
}</code></pre>

**Validating a BST is the classic trap.** Comparing each node only with its children passes invalid trees — a node deep in the left subtree can still exceed the root. Carry bounds down instead:

<pre><code><span class="k">boolean</span> <span class="f">valid</span>(Node t, <span class="k">long</span> lo, <span class="k">long</span> hi) {
    <span class="k">if</span> (t == <span class="k">null</span>) <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">true</span>;
    <span class="k">if</span> (t.val &lt;= lo || t.val &gt;= hi) <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">false</span>;   <span class="c">// range, not parent</span>
    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="f">valid</span>(t.left, lo, t.val) &amp;&amp; <span class="f">valid</span>(t.right, t.val, hi);
}
<span class="c">// long bounds so Integer.MIN_VALUE/MAX_VALUE as node values still work</span></code></pre>

:::deep
- **Recursion depth is a real limit.** A degenerate tree is a linked list: 10⁵ nodes overflow the default JVM stack. The iterative form uses an explicit `Deque` and is what you write when the input size is unbounded — or you rebalance.
- **BFS versus DFS on trees** follows the question: "level order", "minimum depth", "right side view" and "zigzag" are all BFS with a `queue.size()` snapshot per level. Depth, diameter and subtree sums are DFS, because they need the children's results before the parent's.
- Postorder is the one to reach for when a node's answer depends on its subtrees — height, diameter, "is this subtree balanced", and pruning a subtree all compute bottom-up.
- Morris traversal reaches O(1) space by temporarily rewiring right pointers. Worth naming as a follow-up, not worth writing under time pressure.
- Tries and BST invariants get fuller treatment in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q7).

The `long` bounds shown implement a strict-duplicate policy for an `int` key; if duplicates are allowed, encode that policy in one side's inclusive bound instead of silently changing comparisons. The recursive version uses O(h) call-stack space, so use an explicit stack when h is not bounded.
:::

Same traversal, different container: DFS uses a [[o:stack]] (or recursion) and goes deep; BFS uses a [[g:queue]] and expands in rings of equal distance.

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='graph-dfs-bfs'></figure>

<pre><code><span class="c">// BFS: shortest path in an UNWEIGHTED graph</span>
Deque&lt;Integer&gt; q = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
<span class="k">int</span>[] dist = <span class="k">new</span> <span class="k">int</span>[n];
Arrays.<span class="f">fill</span>(dist, -<span class="n">1</span>);
q.<span class="f">add</span>(src); dist[src] = <span class="n">0</span>;
<span class="k">while</span> (!q.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>()) {
    <span class="k">int</span> u = q.<span class="f">poll</span>();
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> v : adj.<span class="f">get</span>(u))
        <span class="k">if</span> (dist[v] &lt; <span class="n">0</span>) {           <span class="c">// mark ON ENQUEUE, not on dequeue</span>
            dist[v] = dist[u] + <span class="n">1</span>;
            q.<span class="f">add</span>(v);
        }
}</code></pre>

**Marking on enqueue is not a style choice.** Mark on dequeue and a node reachable from several frontier nodes gets queued repeatedly — the queue blows up and the complexity degrades badly on dense graphs.

<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Fewest edges / minimum moves</td><td>BFS</td><td>Rings of equal distance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Connectivity, cycle detection, components</td><td>DFS</td><td>Same O(V + E) asymptotic; stack/frontier, ordering, and early exit differ</td></tr>
<tr><td>Order respecting dependencies</td><td>Topological sort</td><td>Kahn's BFS on in-degrees, or DFS postorder reversed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shortest path with [[o:non-negative weights]]</td><td>Dijkstra</td><td>BFS is wrong once edges have differing costs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weights of only 0 and 1</td><td>0-1 BFS</td><td>Deque: push-front for 0, push-back for 1</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

:::deep
- **A grid is a graph** with implicit edges, so islands, flood fill and shortest-path-through-a-maze are these same two loops with `{{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}}` as the adjacency (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q5).
- **Multi-source BFS** seeds the queue with every source at distance 0 — "nearest exit", "rotting oranges", "distance to the closest 0" all collapse to one pass instead of one BFS per source.
- Cycle detection differs by graph type: undirected needs a parent check; directed needs three colours (white/grey/black), because seeing a *visited* node is fine but seeing a node still *on the stack* is a back edge.
- The choice matrix including Dijkstra and topological sort is expanded in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q4).

BFS's shortest-path claim assumes an unweighted graph (or equal edge costs), a consistent adjacency representation, and visited marking on enqueue. DFS can find a path but has no shortest-path guarantee; recursive depth is a resource limit, not a complexity improvement.
:::

When a problem asks for something [[g:per level]] — not just per node — you need to know where each level ends. One line does it: snapshot `queue.size()` before draining.

<figure class='dgm dsa-anim' data-dsa='level-order-bfs'></figure>

<pre><code>Deque&lt;Node&gt; q = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
q.<span class="f">add</span>(root);
<span class="k">while</span> (!q.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>()) {
    <span class="k">int</span> sz = q.<span class="f">size</span>();              <span class="c">// SNAPSHOT — this level's width</span>
    List&lt;Integer&gt; level = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> i = <span class="n">0</span>; i &lt; sz; i++) {  <span class="c">// drain exactly sz, not q.size()</span>
        Node t = q.<span class="f">poll</span>();
        level.<span class="f">add</span>(t.val);
        <span class="k">if</span> (t.left  != <span class="k">null</span>) q.<span class="f">add</span>(t.left);
        <span class="k">if</span> (t.right != <span class="k">null</span>) q.<span class="f">add</span>(t.right);
    }
    out.<span class="f">add</span>(level);                  <span class="c">// one entry per level</span>
}</code></pre>

**Everything "per level" is this loop with a different line inside it:** the last element of `level` is the right-side view; `max(level)` is the largest value per row; reversing on alternate passes is zigzag order; the pass index when you first meet a leaf is the minimum depth.

:::warn Two traps
A `null` root must be handled before the loop, or `q.add(null)` produces a `NullPointerException` on the first `poll`. And on a graph rather than a tree, you still need a `visited` set marked [[r:on enqueue]] (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q13) — a tree cannot revisit a node, a graph can.

The queue-size snapshot is a tree-level invariant, not a promise about graph traversal. Java's `ArrayDeque` rejects null elements, so handle a null root before `add`; for a graph, mark node identity visited on enqueue.
:::
